  "mainEntity": {
    "name": "Hiring a Contractor for a Bathroom Addition: What to Ask Before You Sign",
    "text": "Hiring a Contractor for a Bathroom Addition: What to Ask Before You Sign",
    "@type": "Question",
    "answerCount": 1,
    "acceptedAnswer": {
      "text": "Question: Is hiring a hands-on general contractor the right choice for a bathroom addition, or should I hire individual sub-trades myself?\n\nAnswer: For a true bathroom addition, hiring a hands-on general contractor is almost always the safer, more cost-effective move compared to coordinating separate trades on your own. Unlike a cosmetic refresh where you simply swap an existing vanity or mirror, an addition builds a brand-new room from the ground up. That means opening walls, tying into existing drain-waste-vent stacks, running dedicated electrical circuits with GFCI protection, installing ducted ventilation to the outdoors, and framing level structural walls.\n\nWhat to Prepare Before You Start\nWhen you manage the project yourself, you become personally responsible for sequencing the plumber, electrician, drywall finisher, and tile setter. If a rough drain is off by two inches, your tile installation or vanity placement stalls out, and you pay trades to wait or return. A hands-on contractor like Abraham Becerra at HH Remodeling LLC acts as your single point of contact on site. His crew directly handles custom framing, drywall, waterproofing, tile, and trim while coordinating licensed mechanical trades under one cohesive schedule.\n\nCrucially, a qualified contractor secures all required building, plumbing, and electrical permits, scheduling municipal inspections before any drywall hides the rough work. To review your floor plan ideas and get an itemized project estimate, contact HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866.",
      "@type": "Answer",
      "author": {
        "name": "HH Remodeling LLC",
        "@type": "Organization"
      }
    }
  }
